\section*{An explicit lifted-LMI cone that is not hyperbolic}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 095; 2026-10-05), the asserted result is that some hyperbolicity cones remain outside the class of projections of finite real semidefinite systems, whatever real coefficients and auxiliary variables are used.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
The existence claim is a real homogeneous polynomial $p$ and a direction $e$ with $p(e)>0$ such that every univariate $t\mapsto p(te-x)$ is real-rooted, but no finite semidefinite lift represents the resulting closed hyperbolicity cone $K$. Precisely,
\[
 \nexists N,r<\infty,\ A_i,B_j\in\operatorname{Sym}_N(\mathbb R):\quad
 K=\{x:\exists y\in\mathbb R^r,\ A_0+\sum_ix_iA_i+\sum_jy_jB_j\succeq0\}.
\]
This excludes representations even with auxiliary variables, and is stronger than excluding a direct linear matrix inequality.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 095; 2026-10-05), a positive semidefinite Hankel seed with controlled multiplication kernel produces finitely many inverse-limit directions. Separation from Hankel images yields a polynomial patch whose moment deformation contradicts analytic data that any semidefinite lift would require.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
A hyperbolicity cone without a semidefinite lift contradicts the proposed universal inclusion. The remaining characterization of all lifted cones is not supplied by an obstruction example.

\subsection{Formal verification}
